<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <false/>
    <!--
      Library Validation is intentionally left ENABLED (do NOT add
      com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation here).

      Rationale: build-app.sh re-signs Sparkle.framework and every embedded
      helper (Installer.xpc / Downloader.xpc / Autoupdate / Updater.app / any
      nested dylib) with the SAME Developer ID identity as the main app, so all
      loaded code shares one Team ID and passes hardened-runtime library
      validation without the entitlement. Sparkle's own docs confirm the
      entitlement is only needed for ad-hoc/development signing, not for
      Developer ID distribution. Disabling library validation would needlessly
      widen the dylib-injection attack surface (any validly-signed dylib from
      any team could be loaded into the process).

      The entitlement is only required for LOCAL ad-hoc builds forced to run
      under hardened runtime; those are not distributed, and build-app.sh's
      ad-hoc path does not enable hardened runtime, so it is unnecessary there.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
